From e6270213fa9ba35f91aa0022f9a8b8f1088ad005 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Rinehart <johnrichardrinehart@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 16:56:56 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] Clear the screen at startup without a cursor query

Terminal::clear() reads the cursor position (ESC [ 6 n) so that it can
restore it. The event stream starts before Tui::init(), and its thread
holds crossterm's event reader until a key or mouse event arrives. The
query then cannot take the reader within its 2 s limit and fails with
"The cursor position could not be read within a normal duration". On a
slow terminal, such as the Linux console, impala often exits this way.

The back buffer is blank at startup, so a plain screen clear through the
backend gives the same result without the query.
---
 src/tui.rs | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/tui.rs b/src/tui.rs
index 8d93cd4..7551b98 100644
--- a/src/tui.rs
+++ b/src/tui.rs
@@ -40,7 +40,12 @@ impl<B: Backend> Tui<B> {
         }));
 
         self.terminal.hide_cursor()?;
-        self.terminal.clear()?;
+        // Terminal::clear() asks the terminal for the cursor position, with
+        // a 2 s timeout. The event stream's thread can already hold
+        // crossterm's event reader, and then the answer never reaches the
+        // query. The back buffer is still blank here, so clearing the screen
+        // is enough.
+        self.terminal.backend_mut().clear()?;
         Ok(())
     }
 
